  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 PM CDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nToday through tonight...Dry conditions prevailed across the area \nthis afternoon under an area of low clouds. Most areas are expected \nto remain dry through this forecast period, but, some models are \nshowing signs of an area of weak convergence developing across the \ncentral portion of the state late this afternoon into this evening \nthat may generate isolated showers and storms. Forecast soundings \nindicate a decent cap over the area, so unless some ascent can occur \nfrom the weak convergence, the forecast area will remain dry. \nHowever, if the storms develop, they may become strong capable of \nproducing strong, wind gusts and hail due to MUCAPE of 1000-2000 \nJ/kg and deep-layer shear around 30 kts. Given the low confidence, \nonly have a low chance (less than 25%) for showers/storms occurring \nover the region.\n\nAny shower/storm chances end by late this evening. Attention then \nturns to fog developing late this evening and continuing through \nSaturday morning. Patchy dense fog is also possible, mainly in \ncentral and northern WI. Any fog will lift by mid-morning Saturday.\n\nLabor Day Weekend...High pressure and upper-level ridging will build \nover the area into Saturday and remain in place through Monday, \nleaving a dry forecast for the Holiday Weekend. High temperatures \nwill mainly be in the mid 70s to the low 80s, with dew points and \nhumidity levels at more comfortable levels compared to today \n(Friday).\n\nRest of the extended...The ridge and high pressure will gradually \ndepart late Monday as return flow sets up ahead of an approaching \nsystem progged to move over the area late Monday through early \nWednesday. It is too early to determine the severe potential, but \nthis pattern would favor strong/severe storms. Will need to monitor \nover the coming days.",
